A||||5||GarethWright||Factory Sub Wiring for Aftermarket CD Player||||5||
Z||000000||GarethWright||06-10-2001||03:31 AM||||Today I put a JVC CD Player in my car as I had it sitting in the garage from my previous car, and the factory stereo was starting to fritz a little.  I got a GM Wire Harness adapter and matched up the wires from the JVC Plug to the adapter plug, but when I turn on the CD Player, my subwoofer buzzes Loudly all the time even with 0 volume.<BR>What do I need to wire into the New CD player so that the Bass Control Slider (on the map lights) works, and the subwoofer doesn't buzz?<BR>I do have RCA Signal Outs on the back of the CD Player.<BR>Thanks in advance,<BR>Gareth||||reg||5
Z||000001||Fierokid87||06-10-2001||09:50 AM||||Sounds to me like you did the same thing I did when I installed my Pioneer din 1/2.  You probably have the two pink wires hooked into the power antena ports.  Which on a GM sterio is corect.  But on the aftermarket sterio is not.  The two pinks are the remote, and power wire for the amp.  Hook those two wires together and then hook those to the power wire to your sterio.  Do not use the power antena wire.<P>Scott||||reg||3
Z||000002||GarethWright||06-10-2001||01:50 PM||||ok thanks, which power wire do I wire them into, the one that gets Constant +12 (memory, yellow) or the one that gets power with ignition on (red)?  The two pink wires you are talking about would be pink in the Factory harness right?||||reg||1
Z||000003||btoth||06-10-2001||02:07 PM||||Yeah, wire the two pink wires on the factory harness to the ignition wire.  <P>I tried it and it worked, but with my Sony CD player it was too quiet so I hooked up a cheap 100 watt amp to it and adjusted it so that I could hear that it was there but not so that it distorts at higher volume.||||reg||1
Z||000004||kslish||06-11-2001||03:48 AM||||   <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by GarethWright:<BR><B>ok thanks, which power wire do I wire them into, the one that gets Constant +12 (memory, yellow) or the one that gets power with ignition on (red)?  The two pink wires you are talking about would be pink in the Factory harness right?</B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>You want to connect the two pink wires to the switched ignition wire (the red one).  If you connect it to the constant (yellow) one, the amp will be on all the time...draining the battery when the car is off.<P>If you look at your GM wiring harness adapter, most likely the two pink wires (in the car's wiring harness) are running into what becomes a blue wire on the harness adapter (it is right next to the red ignition wire), splice this wire on the harness adapter into the red ignition wire right next to it.  This way you don't have to cut or splice any of the original factory wiring.  If your aftermarket radio has a blue wire power antenna lead coming from it, do not connect this to anything....cap it off and leave it disconnected.  What is commonly the power antenna lead for other GM cars is actually the subwoofer power supply for the Fiero and it needs more juice than the power antenna lead on aftermarket stereos are designed to provide.<P>Hopefully I didn't make it sound too confusing, it is really quite simple...I'm just not thinking clearly at this time of night.<P>Check out this thread for more info....<BR> <A HREF="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/Forum2/HTML/011038.html" TARGET=_blank>http://www.fiero.nl/forum/Forum2/HTML/011038.html</A> <P>------------------<BR>Ken S.<BR>'87GT and '86SC<BR>See them at <A HREF="http://homepage.mac.com/kjslish/Fiero.html" TARGET=_blank>http://homepage.mac.com/kjslish/Fiero.html</A><BR>Western PA Fiero Club - <A HREF="http://www.westpennfiero.org" TARGET=_blank>www.westpennfiero.org</A><BR><p>[This message has been edited by kslish (edited 06-11-2001).]||||reg||1
Z||000005||GarethWright||06-11-2001||07:55 PM||||Ok, I got it wired up that way, it works great, thanks for all the help.<BR>Gareth||||reg||1
